Chapter: ActLetScope
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A `let` statement in an act block is do-notation: it scopes over the
 REST of the block, not merely over its own `in` body. This is the other
 half of the let-scoping fix. The emitter's locals table used to be flat per
 function, so a sibling statement reading the let's binding resolved by
 accident -- the binding had simply never been popped. Lowering now nests
 the remaining statements inside the let's body, which makes the IR say
 what the language always meant.

 Three things are pinned here, and each one broke a different way while
 2.22 was being closed.

 First, a single let statement scopes over the statements after it.

 Second, a let CHAIN (`let a = .. in let b = .. in stmt`) scopes ALL of
 its bindings over the rest. Peeling only the outermost let leaves the
 inner ones bound beside the following statements rather than above them,
 and they fail to resolve -- CDX2040, an unresolved call. That is what
 `mask-ops` does, and it is how the first attempt at this fix was caught.

 Third, a later let statement of the same name SHADOWS the earlier one
 for the rest of the block, and only for the rest of the block. A read
 before it still sees the old binding. This is the do-notation rule and
 it must hold in the direction that keeps working, not just the one that
 was broken.

 Fourth, a let bound inside an `if` arm does NOT escape that arm. The
 arm's binding is popped when the arm ends, so the trailing read finds
 the outer binding -- the miscompile 2.22 is named for.

Section: Definitions

  bump : Integer -> Integer
  bump (n) = n + 1

  arm-local : Integer -> Integer
  arm-local (n) =
   let v = n
   in let w = if n > 0 then let v = n * 10 in v + 1 else v
   in w + v

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let a = bump 1
   in print-line-uni ("a: " & integer-to-text a)
   print-line-uni ("a-after: " & integer-to-text a)

   let b = bump a
   in let c = bump b
   in let d = bump c
   in print-line-uni ("chain-in-body: " & integer-to-text d)
   print-line-uni ("chain-b: " & integer-to-text b)
   print-line-uni ("chain-c: " & integer-to-text c)
   print-line-uni ("chain-d: " & integer-to-text d)
   print-line-uni ("chain-sum: " & integer-to-text (a + b + c + d))

   let a = 100
   in print-line-uni ("shadowed: " & integer-to-text a)
   print-line-uni ("shadowed-after: " & integer-to-text a)

   print-line-uni ("arm-local: " & integer-to-text (arm-local 2))
  end
